---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import SectionHeader from "../components/SectionHeader.astro";
import Card from "../components/Card.astro";
import RepCard from "../components/RepCard.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
	title="Lead From The Hip — leadership development that measures behavior"
	description="Three concrete exercises for building a daily habit of leadership feedback, before you ever pick up a book, a course, or an assessment. For anyone who leads a team."
>
	<Hero
		eyebrow="Development · assessment · coaching"
		standfirst="Knowledge is cheap. Behavior is everything. Success requires getting in more reps at doing the right things until they become habit. That's the game: Pick the one behavior that actually costs you, then practice it relentlessly. Not until you get it right — until you can't get it wrong."
		primaryCta={{ label: "See how it works →", href: "#method" }}
		secondaryCta={{ label: "Work with me", href: "/work-with-me" }}
	>
		Less knowing,<br />
		<span class="accent">more doing.</span>
	</Hero>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			title="Knowing isn't doing."
			lede="You have probably already done at least one of these. All three stop at knowing. None of them build the daily habit of doing."
		/>
		<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-8">
			<Card>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-2">The book with the secret sauce.</h3>
				<p class="text-muted text-base">
					Five pillars. Ten habits. One framework, guaranteed to work for every leader
					who reads it — as though a weekly one-to-one costs a naturally organized
					person and a naturally spontaneous one the same amount. It doesn't. There is
					no secret sauce, and pretending otherwise is why the resolution lasted nine
					days.
				</p>
			</Card>
			<Card>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-2">The class that ended in applause.</h3>
				<p class="text-muted text-base">
					Ninety minutes, a good facilitator, a room that nodded along together. The
					applause at the end was real. By Monday, everyone ran the same meeting the
					same way they always had.
				</p>
			</Card>
			<Card>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-2">The assessment that described you accurately.</h3>
				<p class="text-muted text-base">
					It was well-built and it was right about you. You nodded. It stung a little.
					Then it went in a drawer, because a description of who you are is not an
					instruction for what to do on Monday.
				</p>
			</Card>
		</div>
		<p class="pull-quote measure text-ink text-xl">
			Every one of those told you something. None of them built anything. What
			builds the daily habit of doing is smaller than all three combined — a
			routine, run daily, not a report read once.
		</p>
		<p class="text-ink measure mt-6">
			Books, videos, courses, assessments — none of them are the problem.
			They just can't replace an ongoing habit of seeking feedback, which is
			why the book goes unfinished and the report ends up in a drawer.
			Build the habit first. Everything else works better after that.
		</p>
		<p class="text-ink measure mt-4">
			What follows are three exercises. Start with naming what's getting in
			your way — it's the easiest entry point. From there, use whichever
			fits what you're dealing with: ask people who see you for the truth,
			or break down a reaction that's still running the show. Small and
			repeatable. That's the whole point.
		</p>
	</section>

	<div class="bg-petrol-soft/60 border-t border-rule">
	<section id="method" class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12">
		<SectionHeader
			exercise="01"
			title="Name what's getting in your way. Then act on it."
			lede="You can't act on what you can't put your finger on. Start noticing it yourself — pay attention daily, then convert the pattern into an intention weekly."
		/>

		<p class="text-ink measure mb-6">
			Buy a physical journal and a pen you actually like. A notes app makes
			it too easy to skim what you wrote and close the screen; a page you
			have to turn by hand slows you down just enough to actually think.
		</p>

		<div class="mb-8">
			<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
				<div class="bg-surface border border-rule rounded-[3px] p-5">
					<div class="flex justify-between eyebrow text-brass mb-2">
						<span>Every night</span><span>2 min</span>
					</div>
					<p class="text-ink text-base">
						What happened today that I'm still thinking about — and what does it tell
						me about how I'm leading?
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="bg-surface border border-rule rounded-[3px] p-5 flex flex-col gap-3">
					<div class="flex justify-between eyebrow text-brass">
						<span>Every week</span><span>5 min</span>
					</div>
					<p class="text-ink text-base">
						What theme keeps showing up in my journal this week — and what is it
						telling me?
					</p>
					<p class="text-ink text-base">
						What is the one thing I'll do differently next week — and when exactly
						will I do it?
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p class="text-muted text-base mt-4 measure">
				Miss a day — just resume, don't reconstruct it. Nothing to write about — that's
				information too. Give it six weeks before you expect to see a pattern, three
				months before you expect it to feel automatic.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			exercise="02"
			title="Ask for feedback. Then act on it."
			lede="Most people never find out how they're actually coming across to others. This is how you find out — name one specific behavior, ask a handful of people who actually see it happen, and keep checking back until they tell you it's changed."
		/>

		<h3 class="text-xl mb-3">Ask about one behavior, not "how am I doing"</h3>
		<div class="bg-surface border-[1.5px] border-petrol rounded-[3px] p-6 flex flex-col gap-5 mb-6">
			<p class="eyebrow text-petrol">Ask someone who actually sees it happen</p>
			<p class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-medium text-ink measure">
				"I'm working on being more concise when I present to senior leaders — I tend
				to over-explain. Would you watch for that in Thursday's meeting and give me
				your honest read afterward?"
			</p>
			<div class="grid grid-cols-[3.5rem_1fr] gap-x-3 gap-y-1 border-t border-rule pt-4">
				<span class="eyebrow text-brass">Why</span>
				<p class="text-ink font-medium">It names the behavior, sets them up in advance, and asks for the truth.</p>
				<span></span>
				<p class="text-muted text-base measure">
					"How am I doing?" gets you nothing back. A specific, forward-looking ask
					gets you an actual answer — and tells the other person you can handle
					hearing it.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="mb-8">
			<p class="eyebrow text-muted mb-3">How to run it</p>
			<div class="flex flex-col">
				<div class="grid grid-cols-[2.5rem_1fr] gap-x-3 py-3 border-t border-rule">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-muted tabular-nums">01</span>
					<p class="text-ink text-base">
						<span class="font-medium">Pick one behavior.</span> Specific enough that
						people would recognize it if they saw it — or noticed it was missing.
						If Exercise 01 already surfaced a theme, start there. If not, pick
						something you already suspect.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="grid grid-cols-[2.5rem_1fr] gap-x-3 py-3 border-t border-rule">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-muted tabular-nums">02</span>
					<p class="text-ink text-base">
						<span class="font-medium">Ask three to five people, not one.</span> People
						who actually have line of sight on that behavior — not people who like you.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="grid grid-cols-[2.5rem_1fr] gap-x-3 py-3 border-t border-rule">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-muted tabular-nums">03</span>
					<p class="text-ink text-base">
						<span class="font-medium">Name it, then ask forward.</span> Tell them what
						you're watching for before it happens, not after — it's easier to observe
						than to remember.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="grid grid-cols-[2.5rem_1fr] gap-x-3 py-3 border-t border-b border-rule">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-muted tabular-nums">04</span>
					<p class="text-ink text-base">
						<span class="font-medium">Tell them what you changed.</span> When you act
						on what someone told you, let them know. It's the only reason they'll
						keep being honest with you.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<p class="pull-quote measure text-ink text-lg">
			One conversation won't change much. Neither will one week of writing. The gap
			between how you see yourself and how others describe you when you're not in the
			room only closes with repetition.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			exercise="03"
			title="Break the reaction down. Then act on it."
			lede="Some reactions happen faster than you can think — frustration, defensiveness, the urge to jump in. This exercise slows that moment down, breaks it into its parts, and replaces it with one specific move you can run under pressure."
		/>
		<p class="text-ink measure mb-6">
			The following exercise is based on Albert Ellis's work with leaders.
			He found they were rarely short on intelligence,
			business acumen, or strategy — their breakdowns came from escalating a
			practical problem into a secondary emotional one. The mistake is
			rarely the real problem; what they decide it means about them is.
			Ellis built an entire model of therapy around that distinction,
			Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (REBT), and the rep below runs his
			ABC model start to finish.
		</p>
		<RepCard />
		<p class="text-muted mt-4 measure">
			No new personality required. Just the same specific move, run the same way,
			until it holds up under pressure.
		</p>
	</section>
	</div>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader title="Who this is for" lede="Two honest lists." />
		<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
			<div>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-3">This works if you:</h3>
				<ul class="list-disc list-inside text-ink flex flex-col gap-2 text-base">
					<li>Have tried to change a habit before and watched it not stick</li>
					<li>Care about how you come across to people, not just what you meant to do</li>
					<li>
						Are willing to practice something small and specific, on purpose, even
						when it's inconvenient
					</li>
					<li>Would rather find out whether it worked than feel good about having tried</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-3">This is not for you if you:</h3>
				<ul class="list-disc list-inside text-ink flex flex-col gap-2 text-base">
					<li>Want a label more than a behavior to change</li>
					<li>Are looking for a diagnosis of someone else, not yourself</li>
					<li>Want a program to roll out to a team or an organization</li>
					<li>
						Need clinical support — that is a different profession and I will say so
						and refer
					</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			title="This is what doing it looks like."
			lede="Most weeks, I'll post how the exercises actually went for me — what a colleague said, what showed up in the journal, what I changed because of it. Eventually, other people's versions too. Reading about it isn't the work. Watching someone else actually do it is the next best thing."
		/>
		<a
			href="https://substack.com/@leadfromthehip"
			class="inline-block bg-petrol text-paper px-5 py-3 rounded-[3px] font-medium hover:opacity-90 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-petrol"
		>
			Follow on Substack →
		</a>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			title="If you want more"
			lede="Most of what's here is free — the exercises, the examples, the Substack. From time to time I take on a handful of clients who want the full version: an assessment, a written plan, and the ninety-day proof that it worked."
		/>
		<a
			href="/work-with-me"
			class="inline-block bg-petrol text-paper px-5 py-3 rounded-[3px] font-medium hover:opacity-90 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-petrol"
		>
			Work with me →
		</a>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
